[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Push Notifications in Xamarin with FCM and APNs"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Implementing push notifications in Xamarin applications requires integrating two distinct platform-specific services: Firebase Cloud Messaging (FCM) for Android and Apple Push Notification service (APNs) for iOS. This tutorial guides you through setting up cross-platform push notifications."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Prerequisites and Configuration"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"A Xamarin.Forms or Xamarin.Android/Xamarin.iOS solution."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"A Firebase project configured in the Firebase Console."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"An Apple Developer Account with APNs certificates or authentication keys configured."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Setting Up Android with Firebase Cloud Messaging"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"To receive push notifications on Android, add the 'Xamarin.Firebase.Messaging' NuGet package to your Android project. You must also download the 'google-services.json' file from your Firebase console and place it in your Android project directory with the Build Action set to 'GoogleServicesJson'."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"[Service]
[IntentFilter(new[] { "com.google.firebase.MESSAGING_EVENT" })]
public class MyFirebaseMessagingService : FirebaseMessagingService
{
    public override void OnMessageReceived(RemoteMessage message)
    {
        base.OnMessageReceived(message);
        var body = message.GetNotification()?.Body;
        // Handle background or foreground notification here
    }

    public override void OnNewToken(string token)
    {
        base.OnNewToken(token);
        // Send this token to your application server
    }
}"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Setting Up iOS with APNs"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"For iOS, install the 'Xamarin.Firebase.iOS.CloudMessaging' NuGet package. You need to enable Push Notifications and Background Modes ('Remote notifications') in your Entitlements.plist and Info.plist files."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"public override bool FinishedLaunching(UIApplication app, NSDictionary options)
{
    global::Xamarin.Forms.Forms.Init();
    Firebase.Core.App.Configure();

    if (UIDevice.CurrentDevice.CheckSystemVersion(10, 0))
    {
        var authOptions = UNAuthorizationOptions.Alert | UNAuthorizationOptions.Badge | UNAuthorizationOptions.Sound;
        UNUserNotificationCenter.Current.RequestAuthorization(authOptions, (granted, error) => {
            // Handle authorization
        });
    }

    UIApplication.SharedApplication.RegisterForRemoteNotifications();
    return base.FinishedLaunching(app, options);
}"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Handling Token Registration on iOS"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Override the following methods in your AppDelegate to capture the APNs device token and forward it to Firebase."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"public override void RegisteredForRemoteNotifications(UIApplication application, NSData deviceToken)
{
    Messaging.SharedInstance.ApnsToken = deviceToken;
    base.RegisteredForRemoteNotifications(application, deviceToken);
}"},{"order":16,"type":"heading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"By configuring FCM for Android and APNs for iOS within your Xamarin project, you can now seamlessly send and receive push notifications across both major mobile platforms using Firebase as your unified messaging backend."}]
